        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(teams): add exact-tab workspace sharing",
        "body": "Refs #105322.\n\nBuilds on the Workspaces foundation landed in #104139.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw's operator dashboard is intentionally single-operator and privileged. Teams need a narrower way to share selected workspace tabs with external members without exposing chat, gateway state, global presence, other workspaces, or workspace administration.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- add opt-in, gateway-local Teams accounts, opaque sessions, one-time invites, and a scope-less `member` role\n- add exact resource RBAC, domain isolation, server-attested agent delegation, and host-bound plugin Teams primitives\n- partition Workspaces by isolation domain and support exact-tab read, request-change, and write access\n- add a restricted `/teams` portal with owner invite/review controls and no operator dashboard navigation or global gateway state\n\n### Security and privacy boundaries\n\n- member sockets receive no gateway-global presence, health, defaults, chat, or plugin broadcasts\n- remote plaintext Teams authentication is rejected; loopback HTTP remains an explicit local-development path\n- invite codes and session tokens are omitted from ordinary JSON/list responses and browser persistence\n- invited users and delegated agents cannot receive workspace-admin or sharing-admin authority in v1\n- CLI-backed agent harnesses carry the server-owned Teams subject only through an opaque loopback grant; the subject is cache-bound and is not projected into child environment variables, headers, or plugin-visible context fields\n- logout, expiry, revocation, membership removal, and grant removal are revalidated against open member sessions\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Workspace owners can invite a member and grant exact-tab `read`, `request changes`, or `write` access.\n- Members use a restricted `/teams` portal rather than the privileged operator Control UI.\n- Requests for changes return to the owning user for approval; members and agents cannot become workspace or sharing administrators in v1.\n- The feature remains local and opt-in. It adds no SaaS identity, email delivery, SSO/SCIM, billing, cross-gateway federation, telemetry, or member access to the normal operator dashboard.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Rebased onto upstream main at `151a259f9a9f6fd96ace336416a3e024477cce8c`; all 21 commits are preserved in order and patch-equivalent to the reviewed `a03df5fd` stack. The earlier conflict adaptations retain current main's inline Gateway client metadata and mobile-bootstrap pairing guard while layering Teams role/scopes/capabilities and member-pairing eligibility into those current contracts.\n- Final-head focused proof passed 285 uniquely routed tests: plugin contracts 12, suspension admission 8, Gateway client plus Teams portal 68, pairing 32, optional tools 85, delegation authorization 49, Teams API 14, and Workspaces schema 17.\n- Plugin SDK API-baseline verification and the complete SDK surface check passed; docs-map generation, dead-code unused-file validation, type-aware UI lint, scoped lint/formatting, and `git diff --check` are clean.\n- The earlier broader changed-gateway run passed 2,014 of 2,017 assertions; the only failures were three project copies of the same unchanged upstream MCP shutdown-timing test. GitHub Actions remains the canonical broad and install-aligned type gate.\n- Bootstrap -> invite -> exact read -> cross-tab denial -> session revocation integration coverage is included in the green focused suites.\n- Independent reviews of security, Workspaces semantics, UI privacy, i18n lifecycle, rebase conflict adaptations, and CLI-harness identity propagation found no remaining P0-P2 issue in this Teams stack.\n- The raw-copy locale baseline is clean, but authenticated synchronization remains required for all 20 generated non-English locale bundles (33-34 fallback keys each); no fallback-only locale output was committed.\n",
        "branch": "feat/teams-rbac-plugin-api",
